  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1111 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nFriday continues to trend drier as the influence from the \nstationary front that has plagued the region with shower/storm \nchances over the past few days moves off as high pressure builds\ndown the Upper Great Lakes. Looking at near to below normal \ntemps to accompany a break in the PoPs for the end of the week.\nHowever, a trough is progged to work its way across the \nnorthern Plains into the weekend kicking the ridge axis east. \nThis pattern will allow for a surface low in the lee of the \nRockies to gradually lift northeastward across the Midwest \nthrough the day Saturday. Expect the low-level WAA and warm \nfront to spread across southern WI ahead of this incoming system\nbringing increased shower and thunderstorm chances earlier \nSaturday. Given the latest track, this would then allow for the \nleading edge of instability to nudge northward into our neck of \nthe woods through the afternoon into early evening to support \nthunderstorms with any activity that develop ahead and along the\ncold front. Currently not favoring strong or severe development\nwith this round as upper-level jet remains to our north and \nlack deep layer shear. However, still plenty of uncertainty in \nthe low's track, timing, and how far north the higher \ninstability reaches, but it is not out of the realm of \npossibility, just unlikely at this time.\n\nExpect the cold front to slide through either later Saturday \nnight or on Sunday, which would keep PoP chances around longer. \nNevertheless, expect high pressure and a drier airmass to settle\nin later Sunday into the start of next week. Wagner",
